When it comes to AI use, I see a lot of commentary about how these tools will either bring about end-times or provide overabundance and ease. There's not much written about what people are using AI for in their day-to-day lives and the practical use cases they've found.

I left the corporate workplace at a super weird time for the tech industry. When I started working on my own in January 2025, most AI tools for programming were pretty nascent, and involved copy-pasting code in and out of ChatGPT. Beyond the UX inefficiencies, the models themselves didn't read from the Internet and it made working with fast-moving tech changes [clunky to use](/open-svelte). Mere months later, tools like Claude Code became ubiquitous, and you could suddenly work like a bulldozer, rather than a scalpel, on large codebases.

I use AI tooling every day to build [Table Slayer](https://tableslayer.com), [Counter Slayer](https://counterslayer.com), and this site. Given the rapid pace of advancement, I thought it might be useful to document how I use AI at this particular time (August 2026) for both code and personal use. My assumption is that this post will feel just as dated as my previous posts on this subject in another year or two.

